## Q1–Q38

**Q1.** A multi-tenant admin agent routes every request — one-line lookups included — through
a `Task` call to a general-purpose subagent before any tool ever fires, and simple lookups now
take 8–12 seconds instead of ~1.

A) Raise the subagent's model tier so it reasons through the routing decision faster.
B) Add a hook that inspects each `Task` call's input and rejects ones that look single-step.
C) Let the primary agent call simple, well-scoped tools directly; reserve `Task` delegation
   for requests plausibly needing multi-step exploration or independent context.
D) Re-model the simple lookup tools as MCP resources so they bypass agentic reasoning.

**Q2.** An extraction tool defines `termination_clause` as a required string. Contracts with
no such clause make the model either fabricate boilerplate or fail validation outright.

A) Make `termination_clause` nullable, so its genuine absence is a valid, honest value.
B) Set `tool_choice` to `"any"` so the model must pick some call that fits the document.
C) Add more few-shot examples of what a termination clause looks like in context.
D) Switch `tool_choice` to `"auto"` so the model can skip the extraction tool altogether.

**Q3.** One MCP tool, `db_operation(mode, payload)`, covers read/write/delete via a
free-form payload whose shape depends on `mode`. The agent has issued deletes while
reasoning through a query it only meant to run as a read.

A) Expand the tool's description with more detail on each mode's expected payload shape.
B) Split `db_operation` into `query_table`, `write_table`, `delete_table` — narrow,
   purpose-specific schemas so a destructive call requires selecting a distinct tool.
C) Add an `isError` flag so a wrong-mode delete can be detected and reversed afterward.
D) Require the agent to always call `db_operation` in read mode before any other mode.

**Q4.** `CLAUDE.md` says "never run destructive git commands without confirmation." In an
unattended CI run with no human present, the agent still force-pushed to a shared branch.

A) Move the same sentence into `.claude/rules/` so it loads earlier and more prominently.
B) Add a `PreToolUse` hook that deterministically blocks force-push absent an explicit
   approval flag — advisory prose has no one to confirm with in an unattended run.
C) Turn the git workflow into a skill with a strict `argument-hint` prompting confirmation.
D) Set `allowed-tools` to exclude `git push` entirely, for every workflow.

**Q5.** A case-facts block states a verified fiscal-year-end date. A later web search
returns a conflicting date from an unverified blog, and the agent silently adopts it.

A) Drop the web search tool entirely, since it introduced the conflicting source.
B) Summarize search results more tightly before adding them to context.
C) Give the established case fact precedence; flag the conflict rather than silently
   overwriting it, unless the framework designates an explicit correction path.
D) Widen the context window so both facts can coexist without contradiction.

**Q6.** `book_appointment(date: string, time, service)` has no format constraint on
`date`. Under load, the model sometimes emits "next Friday" instead of a normalized date,
and this reaches the booking system as-is. The fix must close this even when the model
still tries to emit an informal string.

A) Require an ISO 8601 pattern on `date`, and trust the schema description alone to keep
   the model compliant without further enforcement.
B) Require an ISO 8601 pattern on `date`, and add a hook that deterministically rejects
   any call whose `date` doesn't match that pattern before it reaches booking.
C) Add several few-shot examples of correctly formatted dates and leave the schema as-is.
D) Add a hook rejecting informal `date` strings, and leave the schema as free-form, since
   the hook alone is a complete backstop.

**Q7.** *(select TWO)* Junior support sessions get the identical full toolset as senior
sessions from both a billing and a ticketing MCP server; the only guard is a system-prompt
line saying refunds require senior status. Juniors have issued refunds to persuasive callers.

A) Reword the system-prompt line more forcefully, with repetition and emphasis.
B) Scope the toolset per role at the harness/session level, so junior sessions are never
   handed the `issue_refund` tool at all.
C) Convert `issue_refund` into an MCP resource, since resources are read-only by nature.
D) Add a `PreToolUse` (or equivalent) hook validating caller role against the tool before
   execution, as a deterministic backstop if scoping is ever misconfigured.

**Q8.** A stricter set of conventions for `services/payments/` lives as one more paragraph
inside the repo-root `CLAUDE.md`; in long sessions working purely inside that subdirectory,
the agent increasingly stops applying them once that paragraph has scrolled far back.

A) Reword the same paragraph to be more emphatic about the payments-specific rules.
B) Convert the payments conventions into a skill the agent must remember to invoke.
C) Move the payments-specific rules into their own `CLAUDE.md` inside
   `services/payments/` itself, so it loads specifically when work happens there.
D) Widen the context window so the root file's content stays effectively "closer."

**Q9.** "Flag posts inappropriate for a general audience" produces inconsistent outcomes
across many similar borderline posts, not just one pair, under otherwise identical settings.

A) Set temperature to 0 so identical inputs always resolve the same way.
B) Replace the label with an operational, testable rubric — explicit categories and
   thresholds — so different inputs are judged against the same concrete criteria.
C) Add more few-shot examples of posts that are clearly "appropriate."
D) Run each post multiple times and take a majority vote across the runs.

**Q10.** A triage agent's review sampling was cut from 95% to 1% after three stable
months. A new claim type, introduced by an unrelated product change, gets systematically
misrouted for weeks before anyone notices, because so little is being sampled.

A) Keep sampling permanently fixed at whatever rate validation first established.
B) Globally lower the agent's confidence threshold so more claims escalate to humans.
C) Treat the missing claim type as a Domain 3 configuration gap, not a sampling issue.
D) Monitor for distributional drift and return to higher/stratified sampling for an
   affected segment when a shift like a new claim type appears, rather than leaving the
   reduced rate static indefinitely.

**Q11.** Claude's response contains a `tool_use` block and `stop_reason: "tool_use"`.
The harness executes the tool and must continue the conversation correctly.

A) Send only a new user message with the `tool_result` block; the API retains the prior
   turn server-side, so the original `tool_use` block needn't be resent.
B) Resend the assistant's message containing the original `tool_use` block, followed by
   a user message with a `tool_result` block whose `tool_use_id` matches it — the API is
   stateless and needs the exact block to correlate the result back to.
C) Send an assistant message summarizing the tool's output in place of the original
   `tool_use` block, to avoid resending redundant data.
D) Set `stop_reason` to `"end_turn"` in the next request and send the result as a system
   message.

**Q12.** `send_invoice_email` fails on transient SMTP timeouts but returns a normal
(non-error) `tool_result` whose content is the string "Error: SMTP timeout, please retry."
The agent sometimes treats this as an ordinary success and tells the user it sent.

A) Add a system-prompt line telling the model to scan tool results for the word "Error."
B) Retry the send internally up to 3 times, and once retries are exhausted, report
   success to the model regardless of the true outcome, to reduce noise.
C) Set the `tool_result`'s `is_error` field to `true` on failure, so the model detects
   the failure via structured metadata instead of parsing free-text content.
D) Change the tool's description to note that failures start with the string "Error:".

**Q13.** One long-lived session moves through unrelated tasks across a day — bug A,
then unrelated PR review B, then docs for C — and by the afternoon the model carries
over morning assumptions into the unrelated review, even though useful project
conventions were also learned earlier in the same day and are still worth keeping.

A) Widen the context window so both the morning and afternoon material fit at once
   without the model needing to choose what's still relevant.
B) Use `context: fork` on every subagent call regardless of task type, since forking
   always isolates context irrespective of what the parent session is doing.
C) Compact at each task boundary, condensing the resolved task into a summary, so
   decisions and conventions worth keeping survive without the noisy resolved details
   crowding out and contaminating the next unrelated task.
D) Start an entirely new session at every task boundary, discarding all prior context.

**Q14.** Five independent stock-price lookups for an unrelated comparison are currently
issued one at a time across five separate turns — call, wait, decide, call again.

A) Delegate each ticker lookup to its own subagent via `Task`, since parallelizing
   independent work is inherently a subagent-delegation problem.
B) Combine the five lookups into one custom tool, `get_all_stock_prices(tickers: list)`,
   since a single batched call is strictly better than parallel `tool_use` blocks.
C) Lower the model's temperature so it decides which tool to call next more quickly.
D) Let the model issue all five `tool_use` blocks within a single assistant turn, so
   the harness executes them in parallel instead of sequencing one call per turn.

**Q15.** `get_compliance_rules(jurisdiction)` returns a mostly-static, rarely-changing
rule set; `check_document_compliance(document, jurisdiction)` submits a document and
returns a computed pass/fail plus flagged issues. Both are currently modeled as tools.

A) Model `check_document_compliance` as the resource, since resources retrieve data more
   efficiently, and keep `get_compliance_rules` a tool since only tools accept parameters.
B) Model `get_compliance_rules` as a resource, since it's static reference content;
   keep `check_document_compliance` a tool, since it performs a computation over
   varying inputs and returns a derived result.
C) Model both as resources, since any capability returning data fits a resource better.
D) Leave both as tools; resources and tools are functionally interchangeable in MCP.

**Q16.** A daily batch of ~500 vendor invoices aborts entirely whenever a single invoice
fails validation (e.g., a malformed currency field), requiring a human to fix that one
record and restart the full run, delaying the 499 invoices that were already fine.

A) Escalate the whole batch to a human on any single-invoice failure, since no invoice
   should process until a human confirms the entire batch is clean.
B) Lower validation strictness so malformed currency fields pass through silently.
C) Isolate failures at the individual-invoice level: continue the other 499, route the
   one failed invoice to a dead-letter/review path with enough context to diagnose it,
   and report a summary of successes and failures.
D) Add a loop that resubmits the entire batch up to three times on any invoice failure.

**Q17.** A one-time backfill of sentiment classification over 2 million historical
tickets, with no strict latency requirement, is currently run one ticket at a time
through the synchronous Messages API and is hitting rate limits and high per-request cost.

A) Move the workload to the Batches API, built for high-volume, latency-insensitive
   work at different throughput/pricing characteristics than the synchronous path.
B) Keep the synchronous API but add as many concurrent connections as possible.
C) Reduce scope to a 10% ticket sample, since the full 2 million isn't strictly needed.
D) Switch to a smaller, cheaper model tier for this classification workload.

**Q18.** A five-step legal-document pipeline — gather details → select clauses → draft →
compliance check → format — has fixed order and little branching, but is built as one
dynamic agentic loop that re-reasons "what's next" every turn, occasionally skipping
straight to drafting without gathering party details first.

A) Add Task-based subagent delegation for each of the five steps.
B) Add a hook blocking the drafting step unless the gather-details step already ran in
   the same session, leaving the rest of the architecture unchanged.
C) Widen the context window so the full five-step plan stays in mind at once.
D) Restructure this as explicit prompt chaining — a fixed sequence of calls — removing
   the unnecessary re-planning reasoning and the skipped-step risk that comes with it.

**Q19.** An MCP server wraps a local SQLite file used only by a single local agent
process on the same machine for ad-hoc dev queries, but is deployed over a remote
HTTP-based transport with authentication, TLS termination, and a load balancer.

A) Keep the HTTP-based transport but strip authentication and TLS, since it's local use.
B) Switch to a stdio-based transport, since a single local process on the same machine
   needs no network stack, auth, or TLS; reserve HTTP/SSE for genuinely remote or
   multi-client access.
C) Switch to stdio but retain the same authentication and TLS setup unchanged.
D) Leave the transport as-is; stdio and HTTP-based transports behave identically here.

**Q20.** A developer wants `/tidy-commit`, a personal commit-formatting shortcut useful
across every repo they touch. Separately, this one repo has a team-wide rule that every
commit must reference a JIRA ticket ID, binding to everyone who works in that repo.

A) Put both in the same project-level `CLAUDE.md`, since one shared location is simplest.
B) Make `/tidy-commit` a project-scoped command in this repo's `.claude/commands/`, and
   make the JIRA rule a user-scoped setting.
C) Make `/tidy-commit` a user-scoped custom command that follows the developer across
   projects, and put the JIRA-ticket rule in this project's `CLAUDE.md` (or rules),
   since it must bind to everyone working in this specific repo.
D) Implement both as skills instead, since skills supersede commands and rules broadly.

**Q21.** Deep into a two-hour research session, a critical fact confirmed early on (a
stock ticker verified via an SEC filing) risks being summarized away by compaction or
simply outweighed by the volume of later, unrelated search results.

A) Rely on the model's long-context recall to retrieve the fact from raw history alone.
B) Avoid compaction entirely for the rest of the session, however long it runs.
C) Repeat the fact verbatim inside every subsequent tool call's arguments, whether or
   not it's relevant to that call.
D) Persist the fact to a separately-maintained case-facts record the agent re-reads,
   so it survives compaction and stays retrievable regardless of later volume.

**Q22.** A monorepo has thorough `.claude/rules/`, a detailed `CLAUDE.md`, and correct
`allowed-tools`, yet the agent increasingly "forgets" conventions it followed correctly
earlier in the same multi-hour refactor session, even though nothing in the config
changed. The team's instinct is to add still more detail to `CLAUDE.md`.

A) The team's instinct is right; `CLAUDE.md` is under-specified and needs more rules.
B) This is a `context: fork` misconfiguration — spawned subagents must not be inheriting
   `CLAUDE.md`, so each forked subagent should be made to explicitly re-read it.
C) This is a tool-description problem — the file-editing tools' descriptions likely
   confuse the model about which conventions apply.
D) The config was never lost or unclear; hours of accumulated file-editing history is
   crowding out effective attention on standing conventions — the fix is context
   management (compaction or clean session boundaries), not more configuration text.

**Q23.** A loan-extraction tool with `annual_income: number`, `loan_amount: number`,
`debt_to_income_ratio: number` passes schema validation (correct types, all fields
present) yet still produces values like a ratio of 15.7 or negative income reaching
underwriting.

A) Since syntactic schema validation already passes, no further architectural fix
   exists; remaining bad values reflect a fundamental model limitation.
B) Add a semantic/business-rule validation layer — range constraints on the ratio and
   a non-negative floor on income — routing failures to review or re-extraction, since
   type-checking only guarantees shape, not plausibility.
C) Change `annual_income` and `debt_to_income_ratio` from `number` to `integer`.
D) Lower the extraction tool's temperature to 0 to remove the sampling noise.

**Q24.** For every trivial, single-file, unambiguous edit — a rename, a typo fix, a
version bump — the agent insists on entering plan mode and waiting for approval before
making the one-line change.

A) Disable plan mode globally going forward, including for large multi-file migrations.
B) Replace plan mode with a `PreToolUse` hook that blocks every file-edit call until a
   human manually approves it in a terminal prompt, for every task regardless of risk.
C) Keep plan mode mandatory for every change without exception, since more review
   steps are always safer regardless of the task's actual risk or complexity.
D) Reserve plan mode for genuine ambiguity, multi-file/multi-step scope, or meaningful
   blast-radius; let clearly-scoped, low-risk, single-file edits execute directly.

**Q25.** `run_sql_query` executes whatever syntactically valid SQL is passed against a
shared multi-tenant database. The team wants to prevent a query that's missing a
`tenant_id` filter from ever touching data outside the tenant currently being serviced.

A) Add a system-prompt instruction to "always filter by the correct tenant_id."
B) Add more few-shot examples in the tool description showing correctly tenant-scoped
   SQL queries.
C) Add a `PostToolUse` hook that reviews the returned rows after execution and strips
   out rows belonging to the wrong tenant before the model sees them.
D) Add a `PreToolUse` hook that deterministically inspects and blocks or rewrites any
   call whose SQL lacks the correct tenant clause, before it executes against the
   database at all.

**Q26.** `close_ticket(ticket_id)` resolves and notifies the customer; `archive_ticket
(ticket_id)` removes a ticket from the queue without notifying anyone, meant for
spam/duplicates. Both descriptions just say "closes/archives a ticket." Agents have been
calling `archive_ticket` on genuinely resolved issues, silently skipping notification.

A) Merge both into `close_ticket(ticket_id, notify: boolean)`, since one parameterized
   tool always removes ambiguity between two similarly-named tools.
B) Remove `archive_ticket` entirely, since two similarly-named tools is inherently
   confusing regardless of what each one is for.
C) Rename `archive_ticket` to `close_ticket_v2`, to signal it's a related, newer path.
D) Rewrite both descriptions to disambiguate behavior and intended use — `close_ticket`
   for genuinely resolved issues requiring notification, `archive_ticket` specifically
   for spam/duplicate tickets that shouldn't notify anyone.

**Q27.** A document-extraction agent auto-approves any field scoring ≥0.8 confidence.
Post-deployment, fields the model is actually wrong about get marked "0.9" nearly as
often as fields it's right about.

A) Confidence scores from a model are always perfectly calibrated by construction, so
   the extraction schema itself must be the actual defect.
B) Remove confidence scoring entirely and route every extraction to human review.
C) Simply raise the auto-approve threshold from 0.8 to 0.99.
D) Empirically validate calibration by sampling actual outcomes at each confidence
   bucket, and if the raw score isn't reliable, use a better-grounded proxy for
   routing instead of trusting the number at face value.

**Q28.** A payment-processing tool occasionally times out before a response arrives,
leaving it genuinely unclear whether the charge succeeded. Current logic retries the
identical charge immediately on timeout, up to three times, and customers have been
double- and triple-charged when the original attempt had actually already gone through.

A) Increase the retry count from three attempts to ten.
B) Remove all retry logic and require a human to manually re-initiate every timeout.
C) Retry immediately with no backoff delay between attempts.
D) Attach an idempotency key to the original charge attempt so a retried request is
   recognized as the same operation rather than a new charge, and only retry failures
   that are actually safe to retry.

**Q29.** Mid-way through a multi-hour session implementing a feature across fifteen
files, with substantial accumulated design-decision context, the developer's laptop
crashes, and they want to continue exactly where they left off without re-explaining.

A) Configure `allowed-tools` more broadly so any tool needed post-crash is available.
B) Add a `PreToolUse` hook configured to detect the crash and automatically re-run every
   previous tool call from scratch.
C) Use `context: fork`, since forking creates a fresh, isolated context to work from.
D) Resume the previous session, reloading its full conversation history and state from
   where it left off.

**Q30.** Asked to "add input validation to the function we just discussed," the model
references an entirely different function, as if the immediately preceding turn never
happened. A recent backend change now sends only the latest user message to the API on
each call, dropping all prior turns from `messages`, even though the frontend still
displays full history correctly.

A) Redesign the extraction/schema definitions so the model can infer the missing prior
   context through better-structured fields.
B) Raise the model's temperature so it "tries harder" to recall context from training.
C) Rewrite the code-editing tool's description to clarify what "the function we just
   discussed" refers to.
D) Restore full conversation history in every request; the API is stateless, so if a
   turn is omitted from `messages` the model genuinely has no access to it regardless
   of how any schema or tool description is engineered.

**Q31.** *(select TWO)* `employee_action(employee_id, action, details)` covers
`"update_salary"`, `"update_address"`, `"terminate_employment"`, and `"send_reminder"`
with very different payload shapes and blast radii. Separately, a failed call returns a
plain string "Could not complete action" with no `is_error` flag, so the agent sometimes
assumes a failed termination actually succeeded.

A) Split `employee_action` into purpose-specific tools — `update_salary`,
   `update_address`, `terminate_employment`, `send_reminder` — each narrowly scoped.
B) Convert `terminate_employment` into an MCP resource, since resources are simpler
   for the model to reason about than a parameterized tool.
C) Add a `PostToolUse` hook that automatically retries any failed action up to three
   times before reporting a result to the model.
D) Set `is_error: true` on the `tool_result` whenever an action fails.

**Q32.** A default instruction says to always write in a formal tone. A user explicitly
says, for one email only, to make it casual with exclamation marks, noting it's unusual
for this assistant. The agent writes the email formally anyway.

A) The agent should ask a clarifying question every time an instruction seems to
   conflict with a system default, rather than ever directly complying.
B) This is unrelated to instruction precedence; it's a temperature/sampling issue.
C) A clearly-scoped, explicit, one-off user instruction should generally take
   precedence over general/default system-level guidance.
D) System-level defaults should always override any user request, no matter how
   explicit the user's stated exception.

**Q33.** A response is cut off by hitting `max_tokens` while emitting a `tool_use`
block, leaving its input JSON incomplete, with `stop_reason: "max_tokens"`. The harness
parses the incomplete input and passes it straight to the tool executor.

A) Raise the temperature so the model completes its response faster next time.
B) Add a hook that fixes malformed JSON in `tool_use` inputs by guessing missing fields.
C) Execute the `tool_use` block immediately regardless of `stop_reason`, since its
   presence alone is sufficient signal that a call was intended.
D) Check `stop_reason` before treating any `tool_use` block as complete and actionable;
   if it signals truncation rather than `"tool_use"`, don't execute with potentially
   incomplete input — retry with more budget or surface the issue instead.

**Q34.** A pipeline needs the model to always call `extract_fields` — never respond
with plain text — since downstream code expects a `tool_use` block every time, but on
unusually formatted documents the model occasionally responds with plain text instead.

A) Set `tool_choice` to `"auto"`, since it's the most flexible setting available.
B) Leave `tool_choice` unset, adding a system-prompt line saying to always use the tool.
C) Widen the model's context window so it more reliably recognizes the document format.
D) Force the `extract_fields` tool via `tool_choice`, guaranteeing a `tool_use` block
   every time, and design the schema so uncertain cases can be represented honestly
   rather than relying on the model falling back to prose.

**Q35.** `/deploy` takes a required environment argument with no `argument-hint`
configured. Behavior on missing/ambiguous input is inconsistent — sometimes the agent
asks, sometimes it silently guesses "production," once nearly deploying unconfirmed.

A) Remove `/deploy` entirely, requiring natural-language deploy requests instead.
B) Configure an `argument-hint` on `/deploy` (e.g., `<environment: staging|production>`),
   surfacing the expected argument shape at invocation time.
C) Add a `PreToolUse` hook that silently defaults to "staging" whenever the argument
   is ambiguous or missing.
D) Widen the model's context window so it can infer the environment from earlier,
   unrelated conversation.

**Q36.** `provision_vm(config)` can fail for three different reasons — transient
throttling, invalid config, quota exceeded — but always just sets `is_error: true` with
a generic string. The agent retries all three failure types identically up to three
times, wasting effort on the two non-transient causes.

A) Increase retries to five attempts, applied uniformly across all failure types.
B) Remove `is_error` entirely, encoding failure information only in a text message.
C) Extend the structured error metadata beyond a boolean to include an error
   category/code (transient, invalid config, quota exceeded), so the agent can apply
   the right response per category instead of treating every failure identically.
D) Leave the tool as-is; `is_error: true` is already set correctly on every failure.

**Q37.** A "research and answer" agent handles both quick lookups and open-ended
investigations (e.g., "why did our conversion rate drop") whose steps can't be known in
advance, yet is hard-coded as one fixed five-step chain applied uniformly to every
question, including the open-ended ones.

A) The fixed five-step chain is correct for all cases, since prompt chaining is
   strictly more reliable than dynamic decomposition in every situation.
B) Add more steps to the fixed chain — fifteen instead of five — to try to cover more
   possible investigative paths.
C) The problem is unrelated to chaining vs. decomposition; the real issue is unclear
   tool descriptions on the calls the chain invokes.
D) This over-applies fixed chaining to a task that actually branches on intermediate
   findings — which hypothesis to check next depends on what earlier checks revealed —
   calling for dynamic decomposition instead, reserving fixed chaining for genuinely
   fixed-order requests.

**Q38.** A support-ticket priority classifier is given ten few-shot examples, all
"high priority" (outages, breaches), simply because that's what was on hand. The model
now over-classifies ambiguous low/medium tickets as high priority.

A) Add many more high-priority examples — fifty instead of ten.
B) Remove few-shot examples entirely, since few-shot prompting is inherently unreliable
   for classification tasks.
C) Switch `tool_choice` from `"auto"` to `"any"`, since that resolves the bias.
D) Replace the unbalanced set with one spanning all priority levels, including
   boundary cases, so it teaches the actual distinction rather than one class.

