## Harder Round (H1–H20)

**H1.** `lookup_and_update_ticket(ticket_id, updates)` both reads and applies any
provided update in one call. After raising temperature from 0 to 0.7, the agent
occasionally includes `status: "closed"` on a call meant only to look something up. An
engineer's theory: temperature is randomly injecting extra fields, so revert to 0.

A) The theory is correct; reverting to temperature 0 fully and reliably resolves this,
   with no tool redesign needed.
B) Add more few-shot examples of correctly-formed read-only lookup calls; the
   combined read/write design isn't a contributing factor.
C) Add an `is_error` flag so an incorrectly-closed ticket can be detected after the
   fact and reopened.
D) The theory is incomplete; the more likely root cause is the tool conflating a safe
   read and a mutating write in one call with no structural separation — splitting
   into `get_ticket` and `update_ticket_status` removes the ambiguity regardless of
   temperature.

**H2.** An escalated fraud-review queue's sampling was cut from 20% to 5% after six
stable months, and that queue's own performance has stayed fine. Separately, a much
smaller self-flagged "borderline, confidence 0.55–0.65" bucket that isn't escalated is
only audited weeks later, and fraud quietly rises specifically in that bucket.

A) The escalation confidence threshold is miscalibrated and should be lowered — purely
   a threshold-tuning problem.
B) The two monitoring concerns were conflated: sampling rate on the proven, escalated
   queue vs. review timeliness for the self-flagged, non-escalated bucket. The weak
   link is timeliness on the borderline bucket, not the escalated queue's sampling
   rate — fix that bucket's review cadence, not the escalated queue's rate.
C) The reduced 20%-to-5% sampling on the escalated queue was itself the mistake, and
   should be restored regardless of which queue actually showed the harm.
D) The tool's schema lacks a required "related transaction ring ID" field.

**H3.** *(select TWO)* A shared onboarding assistant serves many new hires at once,
each needing zero cross-session leakage of prior hires' details, plus a shared,
centrally-updatable set of general onboarding instructions.

A) Use `context: fork` from one master session for every hire, since a fork stays
   isolated from its siblings while still letting later updates to the master
   propagate backward into already-forked children automatically.
B) Store the general onboarding instructions in a scratchpad/case-facts file inside
   the very first hire's session, and point every later hire's session at it.
